Claims:
[
    "Drift is a general term for when the relationship between a model’s inputs and outputs breaks down over time.",
    "Imagine a recommendation system for a bookstore trained on 2018 data, where customers mostly bought fantasy novels; if in 2024 customers suddenly prefer sci-fi, the model’s recommendations will feel outdated or irrelevant due to drift.",
    "Drift occurs when the underlying patterns (e.g., user preferences) have shifted, but the model hasn’t adapted.",
    "Drift includes several types, each with different causes: data drift, concept drift, user drift (or expectation drift), and model drift (implied).",
    "Data drift happens when the statistical properties of input data change, such as emails transitioning from 2020 content to memes or emoji-heavy messages.",
    "Concept drift occurs when the meaning or purpose of the data changes, like a whiteboard shifting from brainstorming sessions to daily stand-up notes.",
    "User drift (or expectation drift) happens when users themselves change, such as a platform designed for millennials now catering to Gen Z, who expect memes, slang, and interactive formats.",
    "Model drift is implied when a model’s performance degrades over time due to any of the above drifts, such as accuracy dropping because new query types weren’t trained for.",
    "Once a model is live, drift becomes a silent performance killer because it leads to irrelevant or unsatisfying outputs.",
    "Drift can cause degraded user experience, leading to lower engagement metrics, more complaints, or platform abandonment, especially if younger users expect humor or shorter responses.",
    "Offline evaluations (like those done during development) are based on static data or assumptions that may no longer be valid after drift occurs.",
    "Evaluating a model by looking at the last *n* conversations may miss drift if those conversations still reflect the old user group (e.g., millennials).",
    "Drift can create vicious feedback loops, where users give negative feedback due to expectation drift, further misaligning the model’s training data or updates.",
    "If a user who was previously a millennial is unhappy with a model’s tone feeling outdated, their negative feedback (e.g., thumbs-down rates) will reflect this drift, even if technical metrics haven’t changed.",
    "Drift is considered inevitable in AI-based systems, as users, data, and contexts evolve over time.",
    "The goal isn’t to eliminate drift but to detect it early and adapt before it harms user experience.",
    "Drift bridges the gap between the static world of training (where data and users are fixed) and the dynamic world of deployment (where everything changes).",
    "A model is only as good as its ability to handle the gap between static training and dynamic deployment.",
    "Ignoring drift risks building a system that feels stale or irrelevant to users, wasting resources on unreliable evaluations, and losing trust when users perceive the system as broken even if it’s technically functional.",
    "Drift isn’t just about data statistics—it’s about alignment: aligning the model’s outputs with the current needs, behaviors, and expectations of its users.",
    "Examples of drift include a whiteboard repurposing from brainstorming to stand-up notes and generational shifts in user expectations (e.g., millennials to Gen Z).",
    "The core intuition behind drift is that alignment with current user needs keeps a deployed system effective."
] 

